---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Mark from "../components/Mark.astro";
---
<Base
  title="inixiative: technology for cooperation"
  description="Aron Greenspan's open building blocks for SaaS apps, a workspace that gets coding agents to use what your codebase already has, and designs for cooperation."
  domain="inixiative"
>
  <script slot="head" is:inline>
    // Every primitive's GitHub homepage, and posts from before the rebuild, link to /#<repo>.
    // Those sections now live on their own pages; send the old anchors there.
    (function () {
      var id = location.hash.slice(1);
      if (!id) return;
      var blocks = ["primitives", "template", "json-rules", "permissions", "transitions", "prisma-map", "atlas", "rules-builder", "config", "gloss", "conditional-form", "hivemind"];
      var agentic = ["foundry", "agent-session", "archive", "signet", "kcp", "kingdom", "oracle", "bench"];
      var target = null;
      if (id === "think" || id === "governance") target = "/governance/";
      else if (id === "agentic") target = "/agentic/";
      else if (id === "building-blocks") target = "/building-blocks/";
      else if (id === "foundry-oracle") target = "/agentic/#oracle";
      else if (blocks.indexOf(id) !== -1) target = "/building-blocks/#" + id;
      else if (agentic.indexOf(id) !== -1) target = "/agentic/#" + id;
      if (target) location.replace(target);
    })();
  </script>

  <section class="hero">
    <span class="hero-mark"><Mark /></span>
    <h1 class="display-1">Technology for cooperation.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      I'm Aron Greenspan. inixiative is a decade of lessons from building software, packaged so I
      don't have to figure the same things out on every project, and so you can start from them too.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section story" data-domain="building-blocks" aria-labelledby="building-blocks-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>Building blocks</span>
      <h2 id="building-blocks-title">Stop rediscovering what you need to build.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        On every product I built, I had to answer the same questions. Who can see this record? Which
        state change is allowed right now, and for whom? How does one rule run in the browser, in Prisma
        and in SQL? I answered each of them once, in code, and published the answers as libraries:
        json-rules, permissions, transitions, prisma-map and rules-builder. Atlas, gloss and config are
        the developer tooling I use around them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Template puts them together. It is like shadcn, but for the whole SaaS app: you start a
        product from it and own the code, about 139,000 lines with 409 test files. Six of these packages
        run in production at the company where I work.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="/building-blocks/">The building blocks</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section story" data-domain="agentic" aria-labelledby="agentic-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>Agentic</span>
      <h2 id="agentic-title">An agent that works like you do.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        Then I started building with coding agents, and they ignored the answers. In a repo that already
        had an auth path, an agent built a second one beside it. I kept correcting the same thing: agents
        rewrote debounce wrappers, locks and JSON helpers the repo already had. If agents used the
        template as written, I wouldn't have needed to build Foundry.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Stop repeating yourself to Claude.</strong> Foundry is the workspace around your agent,
        on the Claude Code, Codex and Muse subscriptions you already pay for. Small expert agents called
        Wardens notice what your request touches and point your agent at what your codebase already has,
        and where it's written down. Your agent decides what to use. Giving your work a voice.
      </p>
      <p>I built the rest of the agentic tools for the same problem:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Kingdom holds your integrations and lets you decide who and what can reach them.</li>
        <li>Archive keeps every agent session.</li>
        <li>Signet is how anything uses an integration through Kingdom, on a grant the owner can take back.</li>
        <li>KCP, the Kingdom CLI Proxy, will run your provider CLIs through Kingdom <span class="tag" data-tone="progress">In progress</span></li>
        <li>Oracle measures whether a correction stuck (closed source).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/foundry/">How Foundry works</a>
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="/agentic/">All the agentic tools</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section story" data-domain="governance" aria-labelledby="governance-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>Governance</span>
      <h2 id="governance-title">Designs for cooperation at scale.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        The long-term goal is tools for people, and their agents, to cooperate. The whitepaper and the
        proposals describe how groups set rules, hold each other accountable and invest together without
        a central referee.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="/governance/">The proposals</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" data-compact>
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        <strong>Most of it is open source.</strong> The building blocks, Archive, agent-session, Signet and KCP are MIT.
        Foundry is source-available. Kingdom and Oracle are closed.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="text-link" href="/open-source/">What's open and why</a>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/inixiative">github.com/inixiative</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
